{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RATs9jNhjTL6z4yhhonvsN","type":"tool_result","content":"56:\thdr := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s  pool %d/%d  spend $%.2f\", s.TakenAt.UTC().Format(\"2006-01-02 15:04:05Z\"), s.Pool.Used, s.Pool.Max, s.Spend.RepoUSD)\n67:\tfor _, p := range s.Pause {\n69:\t\tadd(warnStyle.Render(\"paused\") + fmt.Sprintf(\" %s: %s %s\", Clean(p.Loop), Clean(string(p.Reason)), Clean(p.Err)))\npackage pool\n\nimport (\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// Stats is a point-in-time snapshot; the maps are fresh copies.\ntype Stats struct {\n\tMax, Held, Free int\n\tHeldBy          map[Class]int\n\t// WaitingBy counts live, ungranted demand across all loops.\n\tWaitingBy map[Class]int\n\t// OldestWait is now minus the earliest first-denied time in each class.\n\tOldestWait map[Class]time.Duration\n\t// RefusedBy lists recent limited and budget refusals per class. These\n\t// candidates reserve no demand, so they are not in WaitingBy.\n\tRefusedBy map[Class][]Refusal\n\t// WaitingRoles breaks WaitingBy down by role, sorted by class then role.\n\tWaitingRoles []RoleWait\n}\n\n// RoleWait is the live slot demand of one role.\ntype RoleWait struct {\n\tClass  Class\n\tRole   string\n\tCount  int\n\tOldest time.Duration\n}\n\n// Stats returns a snapshot of the pool.\nfunc (p *Pool) Stats() Stats {\n\tp.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer p.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := p.now()\n\tst := Stats{\n\t\tMax:        p.max,\n\t\tHeld:       len(p.held),\n\t\tFree:       max(0, p.max-len(p.held)),\n\t\tHeldBy:     map[Class]int{},\n183:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n518:**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n543:- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n544:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. A freed job slot wakes only the loops with demand, logged as `controller.wake` with `reason=slot_freed`. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n9:test:\n12:lint:\n55:lint-scanner-fixtures:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4188a4ab-5b17-4ed6-a3c5-864fcdaf029b","uuid":"c1f65648-4be7-4602-9a28-4c73e628f426","timestamp":"2026-11-25T15:41:09.605Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"56:\thdr := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s  pool %d/%d  spend $%.2f\", s.TakenAt.UTC().Format(\"2006-01-02 15:04:05Z\"), s.Pool.Used, s.Pool.Max, s.Spend.RepoUSD)\n67:\tfor _, p := range s.Pause {\n69:\t\tadd(warnStyle.Render(\"paused\") + fmt.Sprintf(\" %s: %s %s\", Clean(p.Loop), Clean(string(p.Reason)), Clean(p.Err)))\npackage pool\n\nimport (\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// Stats is a point-in-time snapshot; the maps are fresh copies.\ntype Stats struct {\n\tMax, Held, Free int\n\tHeldBy          map[Class]int\n\t// WaitingBy counts live, ungranted demand across all loops.\n\tWaitingBy map[Class]int\n\t// OldestWait is now minus the earliest first-denied time in each class.\n\tOldestWait map[C